llvm-3.7.1 local rule: an ordered atomic read-modify-write does not modify
a frame object that nothing before it captured
(no upstream state)

This patch CHANGES MEMORY DEPENDENCE ONLY (AliasAnalysis::
callCapturesBefore, which MemoryDependenceAnalysis asks as its fallback for
an instruction that getModRefInfo calls ModRef, MemoryDependenceAnalysis.cpp
:619-621). One change, nothing else:
  AliasAnalysis.cpp, callCapturesBefore: for an atomicrmw or a cmpxchg (ModRef
    for any ordering above monotonic, AliasAnalysis.cpp:335-346 and the
    cmpxchg form above it) the answer is NoModRef when the queried location's
    underlying object is an identified local object, it is not captured
    before the atomic (PointerMayBeCapturedBefore, the call's own test), and
    the atomic's own pointer is NoAlias with the object; ModRef otherwise.
It is atomic-01 without its second clause (a call that may write through the
object, passedToWriterBefore): a call given the object for a nocapture
argument leaves no pointer behind for the atomic to reach, whether it writes
through it or not.
Unchanged: every answer for a call (the existing path); getModRefInfo of an
atomic itself (LICM's alias sets see the ordered atomic as before; memory
dependence's users, GVN and DSE, take the rule); a monotonic atomic; fences.
The library line (3.3) is another compiler.

Why the clause goes (round 596D, scratch d596/notes.md). The base keeps a
frame object written by a nocapture callee across ordered atomics:
  FUN_00c91760's 13 callers: 20 of 20 raises of the empty name on a
    property handle's name keep the index across the `lock inc` and the
    release's `lock xadd` (the handle passed only to FUN_00c91760, which
    writes the kept field, `mov [r14+8], eax` at 0xc91a6b, and to
    FUN_00cb7e20, nocapture readonly).
  FUN_00c91140, the clause's own writer (D93: 0x5f2490, 0x1348360): of 70
    releases of a slot it wrote, 67 keep the index across the xadd (e.g.
    0x293f30, 0x2948ac..0x2948dc); the 3 that reload also pass the slot to
    another call (0xbe0220, 0x13493e0) or compare it (0x654ad0). 0x1348360
    holds both: path A (FUN_00c91140, then FUN_013493e0) reloads at
    0x1348549; path B (FUN_00c91140, then Array<Name>::add, nocapture
    readonly) keeps at 0x13486a1, same slot, same writer.
  FUN_00ed7980: FUN_00ed88e0 writes `instance` through a nocapture
    out-parameter; the base reads it once (rbx, 0xed7a04) across five
    cmpxchg loops and an import call.
The write separates nothing; capture does. 20 of D93's reload sites sampled
(every fifth of e557/s1/R103.txt): 20 of 20 captured (a capturing callee, an
indirect call, the element's cmpxchg, an escape).

Evidence (scratch d596/). The game module built in both stages with the
line's patches and this one in atomic-01's place, against a control built by
the same script with the installed compiler (equal to the tree's module in
every symbol):
  6 symbols change, 0 matched rows among them (0 rows lost): FUN_00e66e40,
    FUN_00e67070 0.68 to 0.72, FUN_00e678b0 0.72 to 0.75, FUN_00e79280 0.52
    to 0.96 (one difference left, the `lock inc` scheduled above its `lea`
    and `mov edx`), FUN_00e77790 0.26 to 0.04 (the kept index takes a fifth
    callee-saved register), FUN_00ed7980 0.43 to 0.22 (one reload of
    `instance` gone as in the base; the one after the spin lock's volatile
    store stays): every one keeps where the base keeps. No draft equal.
  The 103 reload sites of D93: 9 in the module (8 functions, 5 rows), every
    one in a symbol that does not change; the others captured by the base's
    bytes (D93, e557/s1/step1_table.txt), 0x5f2490, 0x1348360 and 0x614fb0
    by the other taker, which the module does not define or declares without
    nocapture (FUN_00be0220, FUN_013493e0, FUN_00613910).

Open: why the base treats FUN_00be0220, FUN_013493e0 and FUN_00613910 as
capturing: their bytes only read through the pointer. Not needed by any
function of the module now; it is the question when one of them is defined.

NO upstream state. Every release from 3.7.1 to 9.0.0 answers ModRef for an
ordered atomic here (round 553E). Sony authorship of this is not shown.

Refuted by: a frame object passed only to nocapture calls, reaching an
ordered atomic on another address, and reloaded after it with no other
cause (MemDep's scan limit, a volatile access); or one kept after a capture.

